## 🏗️ Architecture & Execution Modes

This project supports the **Execution Triad**:

1. **Local Docker Environment**: Run PySpark 3.5 + Delta Lake 3.0 locally in an isolated container without cloud costs.
2. **Databricks Connect v2**: Develop locally in VS Code or PyCharm while executing computations directly on an interactive Databricks cluster.
3. **Databricks Community Edition (CE)**: Build wheel artifacts and deploy them directly to your Databricks CE workspace via the REST Workspace API.

---

## 🚀 Quick Start

### 1. Local Environment Setup

```bash
# Clone and enter directory
cd {{project_slug}}

# Copy environment variables
cp .env.example .env

# Install dependencies locally (requires Java 11 & Python 3.10+)
make install

# Or use Docker to avoid installing Java locally:
make docker-test
```

### 2. Running Tests

```bash
# Run unit tests with Chispa (assert_df_equality)
make unit-test

# Run catalog persistence integration tests
make integration-test

# Run performance throughput benchmarks
make perf-test
```

### 3. Local Pipeline Execution

```bash
make run-local
```

### 4. Build & Deploy to Databricks Community Edition

```bash
# 1. Compile the wheel package
make build

# 2. Deploy wheel and runner notebook to Databricks CE
forge deploy \
  --host "https://community.cloud.databricks.com" \
  --token "your-personal-access-token" \
  --target-path "/Shared/forge_deployments/{{project_slug}}"
```

### 5. Running the Pipeline in Databricks Community Edition

After running `forge deploy`:
1. Open your Databricks Community Edition workspace.
2. Navigate to `/Shared/forge_deployments/{{project_slug}}/run_pipeline_notebook`.
3. Attach your active cluster.
4. Click **Run All**. The notebook will load the deployed wheel and execute the Medallion pipeline.

